CROSSX VOID, twice, and the first cause is now fixed. gemini's suite would not compile against
its own code because `top_level_uses` injected the first line of a wrapped import --
`use farmerbob_core::scope::{` -- an opening brace with no close. That is why crates/fb tasks
kept voiding and core ones rarely did, and it is fixed and regression-tested. The matrix still
voids for a second, separate reason, which I have not found and am not conflating with the
first. No portability signal here, so none was used.

OR-NEMOTRON IS OUT, and glm said exactly why:

  "All three defects share one root: the most load-bearing readers are hand-rolled where the
   spec said import. The marker grammar duplicates `precondition::declarations` and diverges
   (single space only, no fence skipping, no close-delimiter check) ... `run_git`'s tri-state
   overloads 'git refused' with 'no output', inviting every caller to resolve the ambiguity
   toward clean."

Verified: `precondition::` appears twice in gemini, twice in glm, and ZERO times in
or-nemotron. The spec says "Use them, imported. Do not reimplement either rule here." Clause 7
exists precisely to stop "git refused" reading as "stayed in scope", and a hand-rolled tri-state
reintroduces it.

glm also noted that or-nemotron's flagged main.rs change is the wiring the task text instructs,
not a departure -- defending the arm it was reviewing against a misreading. That is the critic
role working.

GEMINI OVER GLM on two things, with the unpinned aggregate exit code vetoed (gemini raised it
itself: some arms clean, others unassessable, and the spec never said; both readings defensible).

First, it models the unassessable states separately -- `ArmAssessment::{Clean, Departed,
WorktreeMissing, GitFailed}` against glm's single `Unassessable`. A missing worktree and a git
that refused are different facts and clause 7 turns on telling them apart.

Second, it closes a hole in a GATE. A newly created `lib.rs` is untracked, so `git diff HEAD`
is empty and glm classifies it `Untouched` -- clean. gemini reads the file. glm's is the
literal reading of the command I pinned; gemini's is the one that cannot be fooled, and for a
gate that is the point. or-nemotron confirmed the behaviour in review.

glm has six more tests and the better architecture note; it does not outweigh a gate that
passes an unread file.

WHAT RUNNING IT FOUND, WHICH NO TEST COULD. `fb scope wave-plan` reports 777 departures for a
clean arm. Worktrees here are provisioned SPARSE -- sixteen entries checked out, everything
else reported deleted -- and my spec pinned the git command without the `crates/` pathspec that
fb-score.sh:67 and fb-critique.sh:96 both already use. All three arms implemented what was
asked; the result is unusable against a real worktree. The unit tests pass because the spec's
own Rules told them to construct `Change` values rather than shell out. Mine, filed, and the
reason a wiring task is worth dispatching: the defect was invisible until something ran.

